<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a conveyor frame?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor frame is the structure that carries the belt or rollers, the pulleys and drive, the product load, and accessories such as guide rails, sensors, and guards, and holds them in alignment. Frames are built from formed steel channel, structural tube, or aluminum T-slot extrusion, in painted carbon steel, stainless steel, or aluminum. Legs, stands, casters, or hangers carry the frame to the floor or ceiling.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What material is best for a conveyor frame?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Painted or powder-coated carbon steel is the usual choice for dry, non-food environments and heavy loads. Type 304 stainless steel is the common choice for food plants and washdown. Type 316 stainless steel adds molybdenum for better resistance to chlorides, salt, and aggressive sanitizers. Aluminum extrusion suits light to moderate loads, cleanrooms, and conveyors that will be reconfigured. Choose the material for the cleaning method and chemicals first, then confirm the load rating.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between a slider bed and a roller bed conveyor frame?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A slider bed frame carries the belt on a flat metal pan, which gives small and unstable products continuous support. A roller bed frame carries the belt on closely spaced rollers, which lowers belt friction so the conveyor can carry heavier loads over longer runs with less drive power. The frame side channels are similar. The bed between them is what changes. See <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/#bed-heading">slider bed vs. roller bed</a> for load ratings.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What makes a conveyor frame sanitary?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A sanitary frame leaves no place for residue or bacteria to collect and can be inspected and cleaned. The American Meat Institute sanitary design principles call for eliminating or permanently sealing hollow areas such as frames and rollers, avoiding horizontal surfaces, using continuous welds ground smooth, and avoiding screw threads, rivets, and bolts in product zones. In practice that means open frames, sloped or round members, standoffs that space bearings and supports off the frame, and frame cut-outs or belt lifters for cleaning access.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">How far apart should conveyor frame supports be?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Support spacing depends on the frame section, the load per foot, the drive and take-up weight, and the deflection the belt and transfers can tolerate. There is no single correct spacing. Ask the builder for the rated load at your support spacing, and plan supports at drive units, transfers, and any point where accessories or product load concentrate.</p>
</div>
</div>
